          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "1+6+7=2+3+4+5",
              "display": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p7-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Partition $\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\}$ into two sets with the same sum.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\{1,2,7,8\\}$ and $\\{3,4,5,6\\}$; both sums are $18$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}1+2+7+8&=18\\\\3+4+5+6&=18\\end{align*}$$\nThe sets $\\{1,2,7,8\\}$ and $\\{3,4,5,6\\}$ use each number exactly once and have the same sum.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}$ cannot be partitioned into two sets with the same sum.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Impossible: the total is $45$, which is odd, so it cannot be divided into two equal integer sums.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8+9&=(1+9)+(2+8)+(3+7)+(4+6)+5\\\\1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8+9&=45\\end{align*}$$\nTwo equal integer sums would have an even total. Since $45$ is odd, the required partition is impossible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Determine the values of $n$ for which it is possible to partition $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ into two sets with the same sum.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n=4k\\ (k\\geq1)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad n=4k+3\\ (k\\geq0)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", where k is an integer.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The total must be even. Start with an equal-sum partition for 3 or 4 numbers, then extend it by balancing each next group of four.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Pairing the first and last numbers gives the total\n$$1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.$$\nFor two equal integer sums, this total must be even, so $n(n+1)$ must be divisible by $4$. Of the consecutive integers $n$ and $n+1$, one is odd, so the even one must be divisible by $4$. Thus $n=4k$ or $n=4k+3$.\n\nThese values are possible. The starting partitions are\n$$1+2=3,\\qquad 1+4=2+3.$$\nIf a partition works through $t$, split the next four numbers between the two sets as follows:\n$$\\begin{align*}(t+1)+(t+4)&=2t+5\\\\(t+2)+(t+3)&=2t+5.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding one pair to each set preserves equal sums. Repeating this construction gives every $n=4k$ with $k\\geq1$ and every $n=4k+3$ with $k\\geq0$. The remaining values, $n=4k+1$ and $n=4k+2$, have odd totals and are impossible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
      }
    ]
  },
